# AWS S3 upload — omit ACL when Bucket owner enforced

When the S3 bucket has **Object Ownership** set to "Bucket owner enforced", ACLs are disabled. Using `ACL: 'public-read'` in S3 params causes:

```
AccessControlListNotSupported: The bucket does not allow ACLs
http: POST /upload 500
```

Public read for the media prefix comes from **bucket policy**, not object ACLs.

## Fix — Strapi upload provider

In `config/env/development/plugins.js` and `config/env/production/plugins.js`, set ACL to `undefined` when the bucket does not support ACLs:

```js
params: {
  ACL: (env('AWS_ACL') && env('AWS_ACL') !== 'none') ? env('AWS_ACL') : undefined,
  Bucket: env('AWS_S3_BUCKET_NAME', 'eventapp-media-bucket'),
},
```

The `@strapi/provider-upload-aws-s3` provider adds `public-read` by default when the ACL key is **absent**. You must include `ACL: undefined` so the provider skips its default and does not send an ACL header to S3.

## Fix — push / PutObject scripts

Any script that calls `PutObject` (e.g. [scripts/lib/push-collection-images-s3.js](mdc:scripts/lib/push-collection-images-s3.js)) must also **omit** `ACL: 'public-read'` for this bucket. Do not reintroduce ACL headers when copying `dev/` → `prod/` keys or registering media.

## Verification

Upload an image in Media Library or editorial-advertisements. Upload should succeed without 500. Durable Cloud image push should complete without `AccessControlListNotSupported`.

